WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.433
Week three.

00:00:01.483 --> 00:00:03.821
What are brands, and why do they matter?

00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:06.120
With Justin Papps from Tenet.

00:00:06.733 --> 00:00:08.424
Start with his definition.

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:13.658
A brand is a collection of beliefs and perceptions that create a positive predisposition at the point of choice.

00:00:13.708 --> 00:00:15.813
It lives in the customer's head.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.868
Why does it matter now?

00:00:17.918 --> 00:00:20.904
In the seventeen hundreds we saw maybe twenty brands a day.

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:24.496
Today it's up to ten thousand, and our brains haven't grown.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:25.900
Brands are economic engines.

00:00:25.535 --> 00:00:28.443
A Louis Vuitton backpack costs fifty six hundred dollars,

00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:32.316
fifteen times a rival that beats it on features.

00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:34.876
Brands equal margin equal power.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.571
They're also behavioural shortcuts.

00:00:37.621 --> 00:00:41.656
About ninety five percent of thinking is System One: fast and automatic.

00:00:41.706 --> 00:00:44.019
That's where brand building should aim.

00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:48.422
Land four pillars: relevance, distinctiveness, consistency and availability.

00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:51.090
And the DBA paradox:

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.842
sonic cues are rarely used,

00:00:52.892 --> 00:00:54.769
but they're the most effective asset.

00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:56.701
His closing line.

00:00:56.751 --> 00:00:58.141
Attention is cheap.

00:00:58.191 --> 00:00:59.792
Memory is expensive.

00:00:59.842 --> 00:01:01.783
Preference is priceless.
